#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Times rind and the libraries it is compared with.

Usage:
  admin/bench-report run [--host NAME] --commit REV --jobs N --target-dir DIR --out FILE
  admin/bench-report collect [--commit REV] [--host NAME]... [--out DIR]
  admin/bench-report weekly
  admin/bench-report render [--from DIR] [--check]
  admin/bench-report gate DIR [--previous DIR]
  admin/bench-report summary DIR

`run` measures the machine it is on. The result's host name is a
filesystem-safe slug of the CPU model (`intel-core-ultra-9-275hx`,
`amd-ryzen-9-7950x`, `apple-m3-max`) unless `--host` sets another sane name.
A machine whose OS has no machine id must pass `--host`: without an id its
result carries no tag, and a name made from the CPU alone is the same on every
machine with that CPU.
The JSON records the raw CPU string, that slug, the logical and physical core
counts, and the OS. Name the file `<host>.json` to feed it to `summary` or
`render --from`. A slug that equals a nightly tier name gains a `-host`
suffix, so a minimal aarch64 Linux box is `aarch64-host` rather than the
reserved name `aarch64`. A result records a machine tag when the OS has a
machine id: the first eight hex characters of HMAC-SHA256 under a fixed
application key. A free host name is kept when another file carries that
tag and is not this machine's marked alias. A second machine that wants a
slug already on disk is `<slug>-<tag>`, or `<slug>-2` when that alias is
taken, and a later import replaces that file, including after the slug
itself is free. When this machine already has the slug file, that file is
refreshed and a marked alias of it that carries its tag is removed. An alias
without a tag may be another machine, so an import never reuses, takes over,
or deletes it. A result with no tag has nothing to make a second name from:
when another machine holds its name it is not imported, and the message says
to rerun it with a different `--host`. A file in the data directory that is
not readable JSON is named on stderr and left as it is, and it holds its name.
An empty, all-zero, or uninitialized machine id is not a tag.
A host whose chosen name is that spelling is left as it is.

`collect` is the maintainer path. It copies one commit to each machine in
COLLECT_HOSTS in turn, waits for room under `quest gate` and for any lease it
has, builds rind-bench there in the commit's Nix dev shell with its pinned
toolchain, and times every operation in OPERATIONS for every library that
offers it, then measures each library's build cost with admin/build-cost. It
runs here for the host this is, and over ssh for the others. A host that is
busy, unreachable, or fails is recorded and skipped, never measured. DIR
(default `.tmp/bench-report/<commit>`) gets one `<host>.json` per host, with
its CPU, OS, toolchain, and library versions. A machine that is not in
COLLECT_HOSTS is measured with `run`, not `collect`.

`weekly` saves the latest weekly `bench` run's results on main, with gh, as
the report's CPU tiers. `render` writes BENCHMARKS.md and its SVGs, among
them the overview the README shows, and benchmarks/report.json, the same
report as data for the site's benchmarks page, from benchmarks/data, the verified code in
benchmarks/verified.toml, and the decision on each gap in
benchmarks/gaps.toml, first importing every measured host JSON of a collection
given by `--from`. It fails on a gap over 10% without a
decision, or a decision that covers no gap the data measures. `--check` fails if they are out of
date instead, and writes nothing, so it refuses `--from`. A gap's `on` may name any host that has a result, and any nightly
tier.

`gate` fails on a gap over 10% in a weekly run's downloaded artifacts that
benchmarks/gaps.toml holds no decision for, counting only a gap the previous
run, given by `--previous`, showed too. The weekly bench runs it.

`summary` prints a Markdown table of every host JSON in a directory: rind's
time per operation and host, against the fastest other library's.

Results written by `run` are measurements, not history.
"""

